2027-01-04T15:45:36  thought   Just the gist, so keep it tight — no lecture. Answer in chat, no file needed for something this short.

2027-01-04T15:45:36  message   To the owner:
A Roth IRA is a retirement account you fund with money you've already paid tax on. The hook is what happens after: it grows tax-free, and when you take it out in retirement you owe nothing on it. No tax on the gains, ever.

It's an individual account — you open it yourself, not through an employer — and it holds investments, so it grows by being invested, not by sitting there.

The catch, since nothing's free: there are yearly contribution limits, and you have to have earned income at least equal to what you put in. There's also an income ceiling above which you can't contribute directly. And the trade-off versus a traditional IRA is that you give up the tax break now to get a bigger one later — which usually favors Roth if you expect to be in a higher bracket when you retire, or just want the certainty.

That's the gist. If you want the current limits and whether you're eligible, say so and I'll look up this year's numbers.

