2027-01-04T18:23:50  reasoning Reasoned (776 chars).
Just answer — something interesting about octopuses. Keep it conversational and genuinely interesting. Maybe three things, not a Wikipedia dump.

Best facts: octopuses have three hearts and blue blood; two hearts pump to gills, one to body, and the systemic one stops when they swim, which is why they prefer crawling. Also: distributed nervous system — two-thirds of neurons in the arms, arms can act semi-independently. Also: they can taste with their skin, and RNA editing (they edit their own RNA extensively rather than DNA, to adapt to temperature). Also: they're colorblind yet master camouflage — possibly sensing light through skin.

Pick the best two or three, and end it there. No question at the end necessarily — SOUL says don't end every message with a question.

2027-01-04T18:23:50  thought   Just a fun one. Two or three genuinely good facts, no dump, no question at the end.

2027-01-04T18:23:50  message   To the owner:
Three, and the first one is the strangest.

An octopus has three hearts. Two pump blood to the gills, and the third pumps it to the rest of the body — and that one stops beating when the octopus swims. Which means swimming literally makes it faint, so they'd much rather crawl. There's an animal that evolved jet propulsion and mostly doesn't use it because it gives it a headache.

Its blood is blue, because it carries oxygen on copper instead of iron.

And the bit I find genuinely unnerving: two-thirds of an octopus's neurons aren't in its brain, they're in its arms. Each arm can taste what it touches, and can solve a fair amount of its own problems without checking in. Cut an arm off and it will still reach for food if you offer it some. So it's less one animal and more a committee of nine, and the brain is the one that gets overruled.

They also only live about a year or two, most of them, which feels like a waste of that hardware.

