2027-01-05T04:00:16  thought   Late-night request, purely social wording — no tools needed. Give a few versions by closeness, plus the one rule that keeps a decline from reading rude.

2027-01-05T04:00:16  message   To the owner:
A few, depending on how close you are.

Warm, close friend:
"I'm so sorry — I can't make it. I really wanted to be there, and I'm gutted to miss it. Have the best time, and let's find a night soon that's just us."

Friendly, not close:
"Thanks so much for the invite — I'm not going to be able to make it, but I hope it's a great night. Happy to celebrate properly another time."

Short and clean:
"Thank you for thinking of me — I can't make it, unfortunately. Hope it's a brilliant one."

The one rule that stops a decline sounding rude: don't explain. The moment you add a reason you invite them to weigh whether it's good enough. "I can't make it" is a complete sentence; "I can't make it because I'm exhausted and also I'm not much for big groups" is an argument you don't need to have.

If it's someone whose feelings you're worried about, add one specific thing — a line about their news, or a suggestion of another time. Specific beats sorry.

If you tell me who it is and how close you are, I'll tighten one down to an actual message you can send without editing.

