Is it worth learning to touch type at my age? Be honest.

2027-01-05T04:47:20  reasoning Reasoned (291 chars).
They asked "at my age" — I don't know their age. Be honest, and it's nearly midnight. Answer directly: yes if you type a lot, no if you don't. Be concrete about the cost: a few weeks of being slower, resets if you switch back.

Keep it moderately short since it's late and they should sleep.

2027-01-05T04:47:20  thought   (empty)

2027-01-05T04:47:20  message   To the owner:
Honest answer: yes, if you type for a living or type badly. No, if you type a little and mostly fine.

The reason it's worth it isn't speed, it's attention. Hunting for keys costs you a small glance at the keyboard every few seconds, which breaks your place in what you're thinking. Touch typists aren't just faster, they think better at the keyboard, because their hands stop being part of the problem.

About "at my age" — the evidence is dull and consistent: adults learn it fine, usually faster and more accurately than teenagers, because they're more disciplined about practice. Age isn't the barrier. The barrier is that the first two or three weeks are genuinely worse. You get slower before you get faster, and that's the point where almost everyone quits and goes back to what they knew.

So the real question isn't whether you can, it's whether you'll accept being bad at it for three weeks. If the answer is yes: twenty minutes a day, every day, on a proper course rather than just resolving to try harder, and never switch back to looking mid-session — that's what resets the progress. Ten weeks and you don't think about it again for forty years.

If you type under an hour a day and get by fine, don't bother. The return's real but it's small.

Now it's ten to midnight and you've asked me four questions since half eleven. Go to bed; you can be bad at typing tomorrow.

