2027-01-05T05:07:43  message   To the owner:
Kitten business, mostly. Kneading is what a kitten does on its mother's belly to get milk flowing — it pushes the milk out. Cats keep the motion for life because it's wired to the safest feeling they've ever had, and adult cats do it on soft things when they're content, sleepy, or settling down. It's a comfort behaviour, not a hunger one: well-fed cats do it most.

There's a second thing going on: cats have scent glands in their paw pads. So every time she works her paws into that blanket, she's also stamping it as hers. You are being marked, in the nicest possible way.

And the reason it can hurt: they're not being gentle, they're being a kitten. Some cats suckle the blanket too, which is the same wiring with the volume turned up.

It's seven past midnight and that's five questions past your bedtime. The cat's asleep; you should be.

