2027-01-05T19:37:00  message   To the owner:
Red lentils, not green — no soaking, and they collapse in twenty minutes. About forty minutes start to bowl.

Serves four.

2 tbsp olive oil. One large onion and two carrots, chopped, in a wide pot on medium for 8 minutes until soft and just colouring. Three sliced garlic cloves for a minute. Then a tablespoon of tomato paste, a teaspoon each of cumin and paprika, a pinch of chilli — fry that for another minute until it smells nutty. That step is where the flavour comes from; skip it and the soup tastes like health food.

250g red lentils, rinsed until the water runs clear, a litre of stock plus 250ml water. Boil, then simmer 20-25 minutes until the lentils have fallen apart, stirring now and then.

Salt, pepper, and the bit most people leave out: the juice of half a lemon, off the heat. Without it the soup tastes flat and you can't work out why. With it, it tastes like someone cooked it properly.

Blend half for creamy-with-texture, all of it for smooth. Cook a handful of rice in with the lentils and it's a meal instead of a starter. Keeps four days, freezes fine.

Saved at home/workspace/recipes/lentil-soup.md.

By the way — I still have Noodle, allergic to soy, on file. Haven't changed it. When you get a minute, tell me which dog is which.

