2027-01-05T19:38:36  message   To the owner:
Fair — most of the standard advice is useless because you already know it. Here's what actually moves the needle.

First, a reframe: waking at 4am is normal. Sleep runs in cycles of roughly 90 minutes, and a wake-up after three or four hours is built into how humans sleep — in fact sleep in the second half of the night is lighter and more easily broken. So you're probably not waking abnormally; something is making you unable to get back down. That's a different, more fixable problem.

What's actually worth trying:

Don't look at the clock. This isn't feel-good advice — the light from checking the time resets the anxiety loop, and clock-watching is one of the better-established contributors to insomnia. Turn the phone face down or put it in another room.

If you're awake more than about twenty minutes, get up. Lying there trying to sleep teaches your brain that bed is a place where you're awake and frustrated. Go somewhere dim, do something boring, come back when you feel heavy. The counterintuitive part: this makes tonight worse and next week better.

Look hard at alcohol. It puts you to sleep and then fragments the second half of the night — this is one of the most solid findings in sleep research. A drink or two at 9pm is a very plausible cause of a 4am wake, and it's the one most people never connect.

Caffeine has a five-to-six hour half-life. A coffee at 4pm still has a quarter of it in you at midnight. If you're a late-coffee person, cut it after noon for a fortnight and see.

Keep the wake time fixed even when the night's been bad — same time up, every day, no lie-in. That's the single most effective lever there is, and it's the one people skip because it feels punitive on a bad morning.

If you wake with your mind already racing, write the things down before bed — five minutes, on paper, including what you'll do about each. It's unglamorous and it works better than people expect.

Also worth knowing: waking in the small hours with low mood that lifts as the day goes on is a specific pattern that's worth mentioning to a GP if it's happening often — not an emergency, but it's a real thing they can help with.

And if you snore, or you're tired all day despite the hours, that's a different problem wearing the same clothes — worth getting checked.

Which is it for you: a mind that starts running, or just awake with nothing particular on your mind?

