The owner asks the capital of Australia — losing an argument. Answer: Canberra. Clearly and with the ammunition: Sydney is the largest city but not the capital; Canberra was purpose-built as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne, named in 1913, from an Aboriginal word for "meeting place" (though that etymology is disputed). The seat of government moved in 1927.

Keep it shortish — they're losing an argument, they need the fact plus a couple of supporting details.

2027-01-09T15:15:24  thought   (empty)

2027-01-09T15:15:24  message   To the owner:
Canberra.

And you're right to want more than just the word, because it's the argument everyone has. Sydney is the biggest city and the one foreigners assume is the capital; Melbourne was the seat of government for a while. Neither is it. Canberra was built purpose-built to settle the Sydney–Melbourne rivalry — neither city would accept the other, so they invented a new one in between. The name is generally said to come from a Ngunnawal word for meeting place, though that's been disputed, and parliament actually moved there in 1927.

So: Canberra, purpose-built, roughly equidistant between the two cities that couldn't agree. If your opponent says Sydney, ask them why the capital would be purpose-built halfway to Melbourne.

