Three, and a spare in case one doesn't land.

Funny: No Such Thing As A Fish. Four researchers from the QI team, each brings a weird fact, lots of swearing and arguing about it. It's the perfect drive show because there's no plot to follow — miss ten minutes for a service station and you've lost nothing. Close to a thousand episodes, so it'll outlast the drive.

Absorbing: The Rest Is History. Two historians, one topic, and they properly commit — several episodes on a single story. Good for a long drive precisely because a two-hour conversation about the fall of Rome fills a motorway. If the car's occupants disagree about history, this starts an argument before the first junction.

Short and clever: 99% Invisible. Twenty to forty minutes each, about the design of ordinary things — why a building looks the way it does, where a city rule came from. Easy to drop in and out of, and it doesn't demand a mood.

Spare: Criminal. Short, well-made, actual crime rather than murder-for-the-listeners — calm enough for a long drive, unlike most of the true-crime genre.

One honest caveat: I can't check their current episodes or whether any of them have gone behind a paywall this month, because my web access is still broken. All three are long-running and well-established, but if you want I can check back once the search is fixed.

