2027-01-09T16:54:22  message   To the owner:
Cheap and genuinely good for a plant person: don't buy them a plant. Plant people have too many plants and are quietly picky about them. Buy the thing they'd never spend their own money on.

My picks, in order:

A nice pot, 10-15. Terracotta or a decent glazed one, bigger than looks sensible. This is the single best plant gift: everyone needs pots, nobody buys good ones, and it can't die. Give it empty with a note saying so.

A brass plant mister, 10-15. Brass rather than plastic. It looks like an object instead of a supply, which is exactly what makes it a gift rather than equipment. Ferns and calatheas people use them constantly.

Decent bypass pruners, 15-20. Not the 40 Felcos, just a solid pair. They live in a drawer and get used for years. A lot of houseplant owners are still cutting stems with kitchen scissors.

A bag of nice bulbs, 5-10. Paperwhite narcissus or an amaryllis, in September if that's when the birthday is. Grows fast, smells good, and the interesting version of a plant gift — it's an event rather than an object.

A good plant book, 10-15. Something with opinions rather than a directory — a houseplant rescue book reads well and won't be a duplicate.

What I'd avoid: a supermarket succulent or a small cactus. It's the "I didn't know what to get you" gift, and anyone who actually keeps plants can spot it instantly. Same for a novelty mug shaped like a pot.

Best of all if you want to spend nothing: a cutting from one of your own plants, in a jam jar of water, with the name written on a bit of tape. That beats the 15 pound pot, and no coworker forgets it.

One etiquette note: this is a coworker, so stay at the pot-and-mister level. Pruners are fine. Anything that looks like a 40 pound present makes people uncomfortable at work.

