Answer: baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate, needs acid + heat to leaven, ~3-4x stronger. Baking powder is soda + acid + cornstarch, double-acting. Substitution rules: 1 tsp powder = 1/4 tsp soda + acid; you can't sub powder for soda in recipes needing structure.

Keep short and useful. Also mention that soda browns faster, and the pantry test.

But careful: this is a well-known answer, no false claims.

