args: {"text": "Baking soda is one chemical: sodium bicarbonate. Baking powder is baking soda with the acid already mixed in and some cornstarch to keep it dry — so it's soda plus the thing soda needs.\n\nThat's the whole difference, and it explains the behaviour:\n\nSoda only works when something acidic turns up — buttermilk, yogurt, lemon, brown sugar, cocoa. No acid, no lift, and what's left behind tastes soapy and metallic. It also starts working the instant it's wet, and it browns things fast, which is why cookies made with it go dark at the edges.\n\nPowder is self-contained — soda and acid in the same jar, dry until moisture hits. Most modern powder is \"double-acting,\" which means it lifts twice: once when wet, once in the oven's heat. That makes it forgiving, and it's why it's the one for pancakes and cakes with no acid in them.\n\nTwo practical rules, and they're the ones that ruin baking:\n\nYou can swap powder for soda, not the other way around. A teaspoon of powder does the work of about a quarter teaspoon of soda, so in a pinch: 1 tsp powder = ¼ tsp soda plus a bit of extra acid (a squeeze of lemon, or swap some milk for buttermilk). Going the other way — soda into a recipe built for powder — gets you a flat, soapy cake, because there's no acid for it to react with.\n\nSoda is about three to four times stronger by volume. That's why recipes use a quarter teaspoon of soda where they'd use a whole teaspoon of powder; use them interchangeably by spoon and you'll taste it.\n\nBoth go dead. Test soda by dropping a pinch in vinegar — it should fizz hard. Test powder by dropping a spoonful in hot water — it should bubble. If either just sits there, it's a mineral, not an ingredient."}
Sent. The owner will see it whenever they next check.

