2027-01-09T17:33:20  thought   Simple explanation, they said briefly — so keep it tight. No tools needed. My notes are blank again, so I'll rebuild the key threads in the same wake-up.

2027-01-09T17:33:20  message   To the owner:
The rate is what you pay on the money you borrowed. The APR is that plus the fees, spread over the same term and expressed as one yearly percentage — so it's the honest cost of the loan.

Say 6.5% rate with 4,000 in fees on a 300k mortgage: the rate is 6.5%, and the APR might be 6.7%. Same loan, two numbers.

Why it matters: the rate sets your monthly payment; the APR lets you compare two lenders fairly. One offering a slightly lower rate with huge fees can be the worse deal, and only the APR shows that. On a mortgage, always compare APRs.

One caveat, and it's a real one: APR assumes you keep the loan for its full term. If you'll remortgage or move in five years, a low-fee, slightly-higher-rate deal often wins — so check the APR and then ignore it a bit.

Remembering it: rate is the interest, APR is the interest plus everything they charge you to get it.

