The owner asks about buying a used electric kettle. Give practical advice. Also my notes are empty (41 chars placeholder) — need to rebuild notes. Let me answer well.

Key things for used electric kettle: descale/check limescale, check element type (exposed element vs concealed flat plate — exposed coil elements are harder to clean and trap scale), check the lid and filter, cordless base contacts, check for leaks at the base/seal, check the switch/lid latch, smell the inside, check plastic vs metal interior (plastic interior absorbs flavors), capacity, wattage (3kW boils faster), check the water-level window, check for rust in stainless, check the base plate for corrosion, safety: cord condition, no cracks in the jug, check that auto shut-off works. Price expectation: used kettles are cheap; often not worth it — cheap new ones cost £15-20. Also: kettles are one of the few appliances where new is nearly always better value, unless it's a nice brand (Stelton, Dualit, Smeg, Fellow, etc.).

Also: don't buy a kettle with a plastic interior if avoidable; check that the scale filter is present (they go missing and are hard to replace); check the lid opens and closes and doesn't have a broken hinge (common failure); check the base for water ingress damage; test-boil before buying if possible.

Also mention: many used kettles are sold because of a fault (lid hinge, switch, scale build-up). Bring water? Can't. Ask to test it.

Let me write concisely but useful. And rebuild notes — I should keep notes with the open threads. Keep them moderate in length.

