What are the odds, roughly, of rolling at least one six on three dice?

2027-01-09T18:39:27  reasoning Reasoned (210 chars).
Simple probability question: three dice, at least one six. 1 - (5/6)^3 = 1 - 125/216 = 91/216 ≈ 42.1%.

Answer briefly.

No tool needed. Notes unchanged (nothing new except the question). Next wake in ~600 min.

2027-01-09T18:39:27  thought   Simple probability — one minus the chance of no sixes at all: 1 − (5/6)³ = 91/216 ≈ 42%. Worth giving the complement trick too, since that's the useful bit. Nothing to record.

2027-01-09T18:39:27  message   To the owner:
About 42 percent. Just under 1 in 2.4.

The way to do it is backwards: work out the chance of no six at all, then subtract from one. No six on one die is 5/6. No six on three dice is (5/6)³ = 125/216, which is about 58 percent. So at least one six is 1 − 0.58 = 0.42.

Why it feels wrong: people expect three dice to give roughly a coin flip, or they add 1/6 three times and get 1/2. That last one is the classic mistake — you can't add probabilities of things that aren't mutually exclusive, because two sixes would get counted twice. The complement method never has that problem.

Handy reference point: it takes four dice to get past even odds. Four dice, at least one six, is 51.8 percent. Three dice is the one that surprises people; four is the one that sounds obviously right but still isn't quite.

