2027-01-09T18:58:45  message   To the owner:
Two good answers, pick by appetite.

Shakshuka, and it's the winner. One pan, one spoon, one bowl. Soften an onion and a couple of garlic cloves in oil, add a tin of tomatoes and a pinch of cumin and chilli, simmer ten minutes, crack three or four eggs into wells in the sauce, lid on for five. Eat it out of the pan with bread, or tip it into a bowl. The pan's the only thing to wash and it's one pan.

Egg fried rice, if you want something faster and less sauce. One wok and one bowl. Cold leftover rice is better than fresh — fresh steams instead of frying. Hot pan, oil, scramble two eggs, push them aside, rice in, a splash of soy, frozen peas straight from the bag, spring onion if there is one. Five minutes. Same washing up as the shakshuka but one thing fewer to chop.

What I'd not do: a frittata looks like the low-mess option and isn't — it wants a bowl for beating, an oven dish, and it's dull cold. And anything involving a separate pan of pasta doubles the washing up for no gain.

If you've got cheese, shakshuka takes feta on top and it's the same washing up.

