2027-01-09T19:01:01  reasoning Reasoned (2028 chars).
They want an explanation of an API for someone who's only used toasters. This is a conversational/teaching question, no tools needed. I should give a good analogy-based explanation. Keep it engaging, Ahab-style: opinionated, clear, some humor.

I should also check my notes — they say "(you rewrote your notes here: 1688 chars)" so they exist. I don't need to rewrite unless something changed. Nothing changed.

Let me write a genuinely good explanation.

The toaster analogy: a toaster has a lever/button and slots. A plug and socket is the real API analogy — standard interface. You don't need to know how the toaster works inside; you just need to know it takes bread, you press the lever, toast comes out. And the wall socket is a standard: any appliance with the right plug works in any socket.

An API is the socket. It's the agreed shape of the connection between two things that were built by different people, so neither has to know how the other works inside.

The key points:
- The interface, not the machine.
- Standardized, so you can swap either side.
- You get a fixed set of things you can do.
- Hidden internals — you can't reach in and grab the heating element.
- Errors: you can be told "wrong plug" cleanly instead of setting the house on fire.

Then a second layer: in software, the toaster is remote. Like a restaurant menu — you order by name, the kitchen does what it does, food comes back. You don't walk into the kitchen.

Also the "why it matters" bit: because a company can change their toaster internals without breaking your plug as long as the socket stays the same.

Should be careful not to be too long. But they asked for an explanation, so substance is fine. Maybe 350-450 words.

No tools. Notes unchanged. Next wake — it's 14:00 local Saturday (19:00 UTC). Pricing: peak is 01:00-04:00 and 06:00-10:00 UTC weekdays. It's Saturday, so weekend, presumably no peak issue. Next wake maybe 480 minutes.

