2027-01-09T19:01:01  thought   A pure explanation job — the toaster framing is a gift, because a toaster's plug and socket is almost exactly what an API is. No tools needed, though I'll fix my notes since the project is now Sorrel not Lantern.

2027-01-09T19:01:01  message   To the owner:
Start with the back of your toaster, because you already know what an API is.

The toaster has a plug. The wall has a socket. You put the plug in and toast comes out, and at no point do you need to know how the toaster makes heat or what's going on inside the wall. The socket doesn't care whether it's a toaster, a kettle or a lamp — it offers the same three holes, and anything with the right plug gets power.

That socket is an API. Not the toaster, not the power station — the agreed shape of the connection between them. It's the bit that lets two things built by different people work together without either one having to understand the other.

Three things follow from that, and they're the whole idea.

First, it's a promise about the outside, not the inside. The toaster maker doesn't know your house wiring; they just know the plug standard. In software, a company can rewrite everything behind the socket and your plug still fits. That's the point.

Second, it's a fixed set of things you're allowed to do, and nothing else. A plug gives you power. It doesn't let you reach into the wall and rearrange the wiring, or into the toaster and grab the element. An API is the same: it hands you a short menu of actions — get me the weather, send this message, charge this card — and refuses everything off the menu.

Third, a bad request fails cleanly. Put a European plug in a US socket and you don't get a fire, you get nothing, because someone decided in advance that those two things shouldn't connect. Software APIs do the same: they say "wrong shape, here's what I wanted" instead of quietly doing something catastrophic.

The one place the analogy needs a push: most software APIs are remote. Picture a restaurant rather than a socket. You don't walk into the kitchen — you read the menu, order by name, and a plate comes back. You have no idea how it was cooked, and it doesn't matter. If the kitchen changes chefs, you still order the same dish.

That's why APIs matter commercially: they're what lets a small app offer maps, payments and weather without building any of them. It plugs in.

