2027-01-09T23:19:24  message   To the owner:
Petrichor — coined in 1964 from the Greek for stone and the fluid in the veins of the gods. Mostly geosmin from soil bacteria, plus oils that plants release into dry soil; rain aerosolises both. We can smell geosmin at a few parts per trillion, which is why it lands so hard.

If you meant the smell before a storm rather than after, that's ozone, swept down by the downdraft.

