2027-01-09T23:25:05  message   To the owner:
Soda is one chemical and needs an acid to work — buttermilk, lemon, yogurt, cocoa. Powder has the acid already built in (plus cornstarch), so it lifts on its own and most of it lifts twice: when it's wet and again in the oven.

The practical bits: soda is three to four times stronger by volume, so a recipe uses ¼ tsp where it'd use a whole tsp of powder. And you can substitute powder for soda (1 tsp powder ≈ ¼ tsp soda plus a little acid), but not the other way around — soda in a recipe with no acid gives you a flat, soapy cake.

