2027-01-09T23:41:21  message   To the owner:
Retirement account you fund with money you've already paid tax on. It grows tax-free, and you owe nothing when you take it out in retirement — that's the whole advantage.

It's an individual account you open yourself, and it holds investments, so it only grows if the money's actually invested. Limits apply per year, and you need earned income at least equal to what you contribute. The trade-off versus a traditional IRA: you give up the break now for a bigger one later, which usually favours Roth if you expect higher taxes later or just want the certainty.

