The toaster has a plug, the wall has a socket, and neither has to understand the other — the socket is just an agreed shape. An API is that shape in software: a fixed short menu of things you're allowed to ask for, and nothing else. You can't reach into the wall and rearrange the wiring, and you can't reach into the API and touch what's behind it.

Which is why it matters: a small app can offer maps, payments and weather without building any of them. It plugs in.

The one refinement is that most APIs are remote, so think restaurant rather than socket — you read the menu, order by name, and a plate comes back. You never see the kitchen, and it doesn't matter who's cooking.

