2027-01-10T00:56:50  message   To the owner:
Honest answer: I don't know how old you are — you've never told me — but age is the wrong variable in this question anyway.

It's a motor skill, not a knowledge one, and adults acquire motor skills fine. What changes with age isn't the ability to learn it, it's the tolerance for being bad at something for six weeks. That's where people quit, and they blame their age rather than the plateau.

The case for it, in numbers. A two-fingered typist runs 30-40 words a minute; proper touch typing is 60-80. If you type an hour a day, that's roughly half your typing time back — a couple of hours a week, a hundred hours a year. That's the whole argument, and it's a good one if you type a lot.

The real cost: for three to six weeks you'll be slower than you are now. Worse than before. Most people can't stand that and go back to hunting, which is why touch typing is rare among people who'd benefit most. Fifteen to twenty minutes a day, deliberately, and the one rule is don't look down — the not-looking is the actual skill, the finger placement is easy.

What it isn't worth: if you type under half an hour a day, no. Twenty-plus hours of frustration to save ten minutes a day is a bad trade. And don't let anyone talk you into a new layout — Dvorak, Colemak. That's two to three times the cost of learning to touch type for a marginal gain, and it makes every other keyboard in the world hostile.

Verdict: yes if you type more than an hour a day, no if you don't. And the ceiling is real — once you're at 60 words a minute, your thinking is the bottleneck, not your hands.

