Let me answer about octopuses with genuinely interesting facts.

Good octopus facts:
- Three hearts; two pump blood to gills, one to body. The systemic heart stops when they swim, which is why they prefer crawling — swimming exhausts them.
- Blue blood — hemocyanin (copper) instead of haemoglobin (iron).
- Neurons: ~500 million, two-thirds of them in the arms. Each arm has its own local control; an arm can act semi-independently after being severed.
- They can taste with their skin — chemotaxis via receptors on suckers/skin.
- Chromatophores controlled directly by neurons (not hormones), so colour change is nearly instantaneous; they're colourblind in most studies yet match colour — possibly via skin light-sensing (opsins in skin).
- They edit their own RNA — extensive RNA editing in neurons, sacrificing genomic speed for adaptability.
- Semelparity: most octopus species die after breeding once; the female stops eating, broods, and dies around hatching. The optic gland trigger — removal makes them feed again and live longer.
- They can squeeze through any hole larger than their beak (the only hard part besides the cranium).
- Blue-ringed octopus venom tetrodotoxin, no antivenin; enough to kill.
- They escape aquariums, recognize individual humans, squirt water at disliked keepers.
- Mimic octopus impersonates other animals.
- Aristotle called them "mischievous" / claimed stupidity.
- They don't have a centralized brain like ours — distributed.
- They use coconut shells as portable shelters, carry them.
- Octopus arm regeneration.
- The "octopus mind" — Peter Godfrey-Smith's Other Minds.

Pick 3-4 best, delivered with enthusiasm. The best single fact: two-thirds of its neurons are in its arms, and an arm can taste and act on its own — so it's a mind with eight partly-independent edges. Second: it dies after reproducing once, and the trigger is a gland you can remove.

Maybe lead with the three hearts + the fact that the heart stops when swimming. That's a great hook: an animal whose heart stops beating when it swims, so it walks.

