Content plan:
- Lead: three hearts, and one of them stops when it swims. So it crawls. An animal built so that swimming is a near-death experience.
- Blue blood, copper-based, less efficient than iron, which is why cold water suits it — and why it needs three hearts and high pressure.
- Distributed mind: 500 million neurons, two-thirds in the arms. Each arm has a bundle that runs a lot of itself. An arm can still reach for food and recoil from pain after being cut off. It's not eight slaves to one brain; it's a central octopus with eight semi-independent ones giving opinions.
- Skin tastes: receptors in suckers, and light-sensitive proteins in skin — it may "see" with its skin, given it's colourblind.
- Never meets its own parent; solitary; dies after one brood — female stops eating, broods 100,000 eggs, and dies as they hatch, triggered by optic gland (remove it, she resumes eating).
- Beak is the only hard part other than brain case — hence "through anything bigger than its beak".
- The moral: it's the closest thing to an alien intelligence we can meet; ~600 million years of separate evolution, similar eyes though (convergent).

