They taste with their arms. Each arm has around 10,000 receptors that respond to touch and to chemicals in the water — so an octopus doesn't just feel the rock, it tastes what's on it. It can be exploring a crevice with one arm while the other seven do something else. And the arms have a lot of autonomy: each one has its own cluster of neurons and can react, grip and recoil without the central brain being consulted. Roughly two-thirds of an octopus's neurons are in its arms, not its head. It's less one animal than one animal and eight half-independent ones.

They change colour to think as much as to hide. Skin colour is controlled by muscles rather than pigment moving around, and they can produce patterns — stripes, spots, waves — faster than a nerve signal could reach the skin, which means the skin is making some of the decisions locally. They use it to disappear against a rock, but also to threaten, to bluff, and in some species to signal each other. An octopus flashing white then dark red at you is not camouflage.

And the one that gets me: they have three hearts and blue blood. Two hearts pump blood through the gills, one serves the rest of the body, and the blood is blue because it carries oxygen on copper rather than iron. The cost of that design is that the main heart tires — which is why an octopus would rather crawl than swim, and why a swimming octopus is a slightly out-of-breath octopus.

Them dying young is the sadder fact: most live only a year or two, and they die not long after breeding. A creature that can open jars, recognise individual people and solve puzzles from inside a jar, with a lifespan shorter than a hamster's. If you want the genuinely unsettling one, look up how many octopuses they've found in the same tank at night, at the door of another tank, with the lid off.

