Key non-generic content on 4am waking:
- The 4am wake is usually not a sleep problem but a cortisol/temperature rhythm thing — core temperature minimum ~4-5am, cortisol rising. Waking at 4 is normal arousal; the problem is what happens next.
- Don't look at the clock. Clock-watching is the single biggest maintainer (conditioned arousal).
- Don't try to sleep harder. If awake >20 min, get up, dim light, do something boring, go back when sleepy.
- The anxiety loop: 4am is when the "worry brain" is loudest because prefrontal control is at its lowest and there's no distraction. Scheduled worry time earlier in the evening (15 min) genuinely works.
- Alcohol: even one or two drinks causes rebound waking 3-4 hours in. Big one.
- Late eating, and blood sugar — a large carb/sugar load close to bed.
- Caffeine half-life ~5-6 hours; a 3pm coffee is a quarter still in you at 4am... actually ~6h half-life: 3pm coffee = 1/2 at 9pm, 1/4 at 3am, 1/8 at 9am. So relevant.
- Room temperature — core temp minimum coincides; a cool room helps, but overheating at 4am is when a duvet gets kicked off.
- Light: dawn simulation — if the wake is consistent at 4am and they fall back asleep, fine. If they can't, it's possibly advanced sleep phase (common with age) — actually waking at 4 and being unable to return can be a phase advance.
- The "don't try to sleep" paradox / paradoxical intention: genuinely evidence-based.
- If it's every night at exactly 4am and they feel awful, and there's snoring/breathing stuff, consider sleep apnoea — that's worth a doctor. Also 4am waking with dread = check mood.
- CBT-I is the actual evidence-based treatment for chronic insomnia; better than any app.

