2027-01-10T12:35:41  message   To the owner:
You asked me this on Tuesday and I gave you the long version, so here's the one that's actually different — and it starts with the thing nobody says: waking at 4am is normal. Staying awake at 4am is the problem, and it has a specific cause you can go at.

Why 4am specifically. Your core temperature hits its daily low around 4 to 5, and cortisol starts climbing from about 3. That combination makes you lighter-sleeping at exactly that hour, so anything that half-wakes you — a noise, a full bladder, a temperature change — lands while you're already near the surface. Everyone wakes briefly in the night. Yours keep happening to coincide with the hour you're most likely to notice.

So the useful question isn't "why do I wake" but "why don't I go back". Usually one of these:

The clock. If you check the time, you've told your brain that 4am is an event worth being awake for. Clock-turners train themselves awake. Turn it round, cover it, phone face-down and out of reach. This is the single highest-yield change and it sounds too stupid to work.

Trying. Effort is the enemy here. "I must get back to sleep" is a performance demand, and your body responds to performance demands the way it does before a speech. If you're still awake after twenty minutes, get up. Dim light, boring chair, no phone, nothing interesting. Go back when you're actually heavy-eyed. Lying there grinding is what teaches your brain that bed is a place to be frustrated.

The 4am brain. Prefrontal control is at its daily low and there's no distraction, so whatever you're carrying shows up at full volume, rearranged into something worse. That's chemistry, not insight — nothing decided at 4am is more true than the same thing at 2pm. The practical fix is a fifteen-minute worry window at about six in the evening: write down what's circling and one next step for each. It sounds twee and it measurably reduces night waking, because the brain stops treating 4am as its only appointment.

Then the physical levers, in order of how much they matter:

Alcohol. Even two drinks reliably cause a lighter second half of the night, three to four hours in — which for an evening drinker is 3 to 4am on the nose. If you drink most evenings, this is likely your answer and it's worth a fortnight off to find out.

Caffeine after about two in the afternoon. Half-life is five to six hours, so a 3pm coffee is still a quarter of itself in you at 3am. It doesn't stop you falling asleep; it shortens what you get.

Eating late, especially sugar or a big plate of carbs. Blood sugar swings wake people in the early hours. Three hours between dinner and bed is the usual fix.

A warm room. You're dumping heat at 4am — if the duvet's kicked off when you wake, that's the reason, and a cooler room or a lighter duvet is worth trying before anything cleverer.

One thing not to bother with: sleep trackers. They measure movement, not sleep, and they mostly generate anxiety about a number that isn't real. If you want to track anything, track what time you woke and what you'd had to drink, on paper.

And the honest bit. If it's been every night for more than a month, if you're snoring or someone's told you that you stop breathing, if you're falling asleep in the day, or if the 4am waking comes with a flat dread that doesn't lift by mid-morning — those are worth a GP rather than a list. The dread-in-the-early-hours pattern in particular is a well-known thing and it's treatable. For straightforward chronic insomnia, CBT-I is the thing with the evidence behind it, far more than any app.

One personal note, gently: you asked me this on Tuesday, and last night was forty questions and two good nights before nine. If this has been building for a week or two rather than being a long-standing thing, it's probably the same cause as the scattered day, and it'll settle when whatever's underneath settles.

Want me to write this up as a file so it's not a wall of text you have to scroll back for?

