2027-01-10T12:48:33  message   To the owner:
Nobody knows, and that's the first honest thing to say — it's a behaviour with several plausible explanations and no way to test them properly.

The best one is that it's leftover kitten behaviour. Kittens knead their mother's belly to stimulate milk flow, and the motion is present from the first days of life. Most cats grow out of it as the milk stops mattering; some don't, and keep the pattern into adulthood the way people keep sucking their thumb or biting their nails. The fact that kneading usually comes with purring, half-closed eyes and a kneaded blanket or lap — the softest thing available — points at it being self-soothing rather than anything deliberate.

Which is why it lands on you, of all things: you're warm, soft, and you're the one who feeds them. A cat kneading your stomach in bed is not doing you a kindness; you're a substitute mother. The drooling that sometimes comes with it is the same mistake, in that the nursing reflex is fully engaged.

The other theories, in order of how much I believe them:

Scent marking. Cats have scent glands between their toes, so kneading leaves your smell mixed with their own — territory marking that reads as affection because that's how cats show it. This is genuine, but I think it's a side effect rather than the cause.

Nesting. They do it to grass and leaves before lying down, in the wild and in shelters. Circling, kneading, then lying down is the same sequence. Plausible for some cats and not others, since it usually comes with circling first.

Comfort, plain and simple. The honest catch-all. It feels good, it's what they've always done, and cats keep doing things that feel good.

One practical note: those claws are real, so if your stomach or your best blanket is involved, a folded throw over your lap is the diplomatic answer. And if a cat suddenly starts kneading a lot as an older animal, especially in the air while sitting in your lap, that can be pain — worth a vet rather than a shrug.

