2027-01-10T15:26:12  message   To the owner:
Paint it — but only if the problem is actually the paint. That's the test, and it's worth two minutes before you buy anything.

If the walls look grubby, scuffed, or yellowed, that's real and repainting genuinely fixes it. Kitchens get a film of cooking grease on everything, and no amount of cleaning brings a 15-year-old magnolia back. In a room you're in every day, that's one of the best-value weekends you can spend.

If the problem is that the kitchen feels tired, dark or boring, paint is usually the wrong tool and you'll be disappointed a week later. In kitchens the culprits are almost always, in order: the lighting (one ceiling bulb and no under-cabinet light makes any colour look grim), the hardware (handles, and a tap), and clutter on the worktop. I'd do those three before I picked up a brush. New handles and a decent bulb cost less than the paint and take an afternoon.

The renter question, before anything else — I know you've got a landlord because of the hallway light. If you rent, check the tenancy or just ask. Most say you can't paint without permission, and the cheap ones say you must put it back. Getting that in writing by text takes a minute, and doing it without asking risks the deposit over a colour they'd probably have said yes to.

If you go ahead, three things that matter more than the colour:

Degrease first. Sugar soap, every surface, twice near the hob. Paint won't stick to kitchen grease and it'll flake within months — this is why people say painting kitchens is awful.

Eggshell or a scrubbable finish, never matt. Matt in a kitchen is a sponge. The trade-off is a slight sheen, which is fine.

And pick something with a bit of colour. Pure white in a kitchen shows every splash, every fingerprint, and looks clinical under a single bulb. A soft green, a warm off-white, or a pale grey all wear far better.

So: yes, repaint, if it's grubby. If it's boring, change the handles and the light first and see if you still want to. What's making you ask — the state of the walls or the look of the room?

