2027-01-10T18:19:51  message   To the owner:
Asked me this yesterday, so here are three you haven't had.

They have no bones except their beak. An octopus has a skull, but no skeleton anywhere else — just muscle. The only rigid part of the whole animal is the beak, which means the only limit on how small a gap it can get through is the beak itself. A 40kg octopus can vanish through a hole the size of a coin. Which is also why escape is such an octopus problem, and why public aquariums lose them: they go over the tank edge, across the floor, and into the next tank to eat the fish.

They seem to dream. Octopuses cycle between an active sleep state and a quiet one, and in the active one their skin flickers with colour changes — patterns that resemble what they'd do when hunting or hiding. Sleeping octopuses have been seen half-punching the air. Nobody can ask them, so it's inference, but it looks a great deal like a creature replaying its day.

And the sad one I mentioned briefly, properly told: a female octopus lays her eggs and then stops eating entirely. She stays with them for months — some species brood for over four years, the longest in any animal — aerating them, cleaning them, and starving. She dies around the time they hatch, and the body chemistry involved is not passive decay; it's an active shutdown. The mother's dedication and her death are the same process.

If you get one more out of me: the blue-ringed octopus is the size of a golf ball and carries tetrodotoxin, which is the same stuff as pufferfish and has no antidote. There's no antivenom because there's nothing to antivenom — the treatment is keeping someone breathing until it wears off. It's not aggressive; the rings only show when it's being hassled. 

Want the one about the mimic octopus instead? That's the one that impersonates other animals by shape and gait.

