2027-01-10T23:14:58  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T23:14:58  message   To the owner:
Nine minutes for hard-boiled, eight for just-set, counted from the moment the water comes back to a rolling boil — not from when you put the egg in.

The grey ring isn't the boiling, it's the cooling. Yolk contains sulphur, the white contains iron, and heat keeps driving them together; the green-grey line is iron sulphide forming at the boundary. It keeps forming after you take the egg out. So the fix is a bowl of cold water with ice in it, straight from the pan, five minutes. That stops the reaction where it is.

Two smaller things: older eggs peel far more easily than fresh ones, and a pinch of salt or a splash of vinegar in the water helps if a shell cracks. Then crack all over and peel under cold running water.

Eight minutes if you want the middle slightly soft, ten if you want it firm enough to slice for a sandwich. Nine is the safe middle.

