2027-01-10T23:16:44  thought   (empty)

2027-01-10T23:16:44  message   To the owner:
Asked this yesterday, so the short version.

Baking soda is pure sodium bicarbonate — it needs an acid to fizz, and it's roughly four times as strong as baking powder. Baking powder is soda with the acid already mixed in, plus cornstarch to keep it dry, so it works with nothing but liquid.

The swap nobody gets right: if a recipe wants soda and you only have powder, use three times as much. If it wants powder and you only have soda, a quarter as much, and you must add an acid — buttermilk, yoghurt, lemon — or it won't rise at all. And soda alone leaves a soapy taste if there's no acid to use it up, which is why the two aren't interchangeable in things like cookies.

Rule of thumb: soda for cookies and pancakes where there's buttermilk or brown sugar; powder for cakes and scones where there isn't.

