2027-01-10T23:22:23  message   To the owner:
Asked me this morning, so the short version.

Paint it if the walls are grubby, scuffed or yellowed — kitchen grease and time do that, cleaning won't fix it, and it's a genuinely good weekend's work.

Don't paint it if the kitchen just feels tired. In kitchens the culprit is almost always the lighting, the handles, or clutter on the worktop, and paint won't touch any of those. New handles and a proper bulb under the cabinets cost less than the paint.

If you rent, get permission in a text first — the landlord with the hallway light won't mind a colour half as much as he'd mind being surprised.

If you do it: degrease every surface twice near the hob (paint won't stick to grease), use eggshell rather than matt, and pick something with a bit of colour — pure white in a kitchen shows every splash.

What's actually bothering you about it, the state of the walls or the look of the room?

