2027-01-11T01:09:23  message   To the owner:
Answered this properly this morning, so the short version rather than the essay again.

Waking at 4am is normal — core temperature hits its low and you're near the surface. The problem is going back, and there are four things that fix that better than anything else:

Don't look at the clock. Cover it, turn it round, phone out of reach. Checking the time teaches your brain that 4am is worth waking for, and it's the single biggest one.

Don't try. If you're still awake after twenty minutes, get up and sit somewhere boring in dim light. Lying there working at it is what trains the frustration in.

The 4am brain is not insightful, it's just unopposed. Whatever it tells you at that hour, write it down in the evening instead — fifteen minutes around six, one next step per worry.

And the physical levers, in order: alcohol (two drinks reliably lightens the second half of the night, which lands at 3-4am), caffeine after 2pm, a warm room, eating late.

If it's every night for more than a month, or there's snoring or daytime sleepiness with it, that's a GP rather than a list.

Want it as a file so you're not scrolling back for it at four in the morning? Say the word and I'll write it out properly — the whole thing, not this.

