2027-01-11T01:24:18  message   To the owner:
Petrichor — the word I offered you for the playlist.

Coined in 1964 by two Australian researchers, Bear and Thomas, in a paper in Nature. They built it from the Greek petra, stone, and ichor, the fluid that runs in the veins of the gods — so literally "the blood of stones". They were studying the smell of rain on clay and dry soil and needed a word for it.

What you're actually smelling is a mix of things. The main one is geosmin, made by soil bacteria, which your nose detects at about five parts per trillion — a sensitivity so absurd it's thought to be why the smell is so evocative. There's also a plant oil that builds up on rocks and soil during dry spells and gets released when water hits it, plus ozone, which the wind brings down ahead of a storm and which is the smell people mean when they say they can smell rain coming.

Why it travels: raindrops hitting porous ground trap tiny air bubbles that shoot upward and burst at the surface, throwing out aerosols. That's why the smell arrives in a rush rather than gradually.

And the reason it feels like memory rather than smell: geosmin is also what makes beetroot taste of earth, and it's produced by the same bacteria that give soil its smell. You're recognising the ground, not the weather.

