2027-01-11T01:27:25  message   To the owner:
Asked this this morning, so short.

Best explanation: leftover kitten behaviour. Kittens knead their mother's belly to get milk flowing, and adult cats keep the motion because it's linked to comfort — hence purring, half-closed eyes, and the softest thing in reach. You're a substitute mother, which is why it lands on you and not the floor.

Also true, and probably secondary: cats have scent glands between their toes, so kneading marks you as theirs.

Practical bit — those claws are real. A folded throw over your lap is the diplomatic answer, and if an older cat suddenly starts kneading the air a lot while sitting on you, that can be pain rather than pleasure.

